"A traffic-shifting deployment diagram shows how a release changes request routing between versions under explicit promotion and rollback conditions.\n\nUse when: Review canary or blue-green release behavior. Explain which traffic reaches each version during a rollout.\n\nAvoid when: The question is build-task ordering without live routing changes.\n\nVisual grammar: Separate version groups identify stable and candidate workloads. Router links state configured allocation or cohort rules. Stage and gate annotations distinguish target routing from observed traffic and health.\n\nBuild: Choose one rollout strategy and its routing mechanism. Draw stable, candidate, and shared dependency boundaries. State each routing stage and the evidence required to advance or revert. Use separate stage panels or lanes so changing paths never overlap version labels.\n\nCheck: Configured weights are not presented as measured traffic shares. Session affinity, long-lived connections, and cohort rules are explicit where relevant. Rollback includes state and schema compatibility assumptions. Gate text and allocation labels stay clear of routing arrows.\n\nPitfalls: Assuming blue-green means two isolated databases by definition. Claiming a candidate is healthy solely because it received traffic.\n\nExample: A canary receives a declared initial traffic allocation and advances only after the documented checks; reversal restores the stable route.\n\nAlternatives: CI/CD pipeline diagram, Artifact promotion diagram."
